#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1576ea0-ab01-5d32-81b1-782024bdcc98" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Additive Inverses Modulo N"}] :question/id #uuid "b12c927f-5cfd-5c8d-876e-6995d17d2a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-14655" :question/problem "Is the following statement true or false?\n\nThe integer $21$ is the additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35.$" :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 35$ such that\n\n$$\nx + 14 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:35).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $35{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21+14 & ≡35 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35) \\\\  & ≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the statement is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5513a4ce-730e-56fd-ac8c-8ee1b002abfc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "b7f4fa4a-b123-514d-ae6e-6482805e0168" :question/math-academy-id "e-14659" :question/problem "Find the additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26.$" :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 26$ such that\n\n$$\nx + 17 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-17 + 17 \\equiv 0,$ so $x\\equiv -17\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:26).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-17 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26) \\\\  & ≡0-17 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26) \\\\  & ≡26-17 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26) \\\\  & ≡9 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $17$ modulo $26$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n9 + 17 \\equiv 26 \\equiv 0\\:\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/id #uuid "25638e23-0a57-53d6-9451-ff1cf133d80e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14660" :question/problem "Find the additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15.$" :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 15$ such that\n\n$$\nx + 23 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $23\\equiv 8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23 & ≡1\\cdot 15+8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-8 + 8 \\equiv 0,$ so $x\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡0-8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡15-8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡7 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $23$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23+7≡30≡2\\cdot 15≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c91373a9-4a84-52ea-a77a-16beabf09acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "e68d2ba3-b6d2-5906-a8d0-f22b70eeb171" :question/math-academy-id "e-14661" :question/problem "Find the additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15.$" :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 15$ such that\n\n$$\nx + (-43) \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-43\\equiv 2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-43 & ≡(-3)\\cdot 15+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡(-3)\\cdot 0+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-2 + 2 \\equiv 0,$ so $x\\equiv -2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡0-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡15-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-43$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}13}.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-43+13≡-30≡(-2)\\cdot 15≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5622e87e-a13c-521d-a9f8-7480f0fe6803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5622e87e-a13c-521d-a9f8-7480f0fe6803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415328} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5bcda9f-0da1-5748-9b38-9b92a07998f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29a5c0fc-899a-52da-8f72-1b9fc79f6f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-213236" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16,$ the additive inverse of $45$ modulo $16$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $45$ modulo $16$ is the integer $0\\le x<16$ such that\n\n$$\nx+45≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $45≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45 & ≡2\\cdot 16+13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-13+13≡0,$ so $x≡-13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡0-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡16-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $45$ modulo $16$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45+3≡48≡3\\cdot 16≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c91373a9-4a84-52ea-a77a-16beabf09acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa214343-d046-582c-82b3-c94cfea94052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa214343-d046-582c-82b3-c94cfea94052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415344} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b63bca3-54e7-56c6-bed9-1bfe3820316e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0337befa-d550-5b7b-a108-eacd390b3246" :question/math-academy-id "q-213239" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13,$ the additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is the integer $0\\le x<13$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-50)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-50≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & ≡(-4)\\cdot 13+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡(-4)\\cdot 0+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-2+2≡0,$ so $x≡-2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡0-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡13-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡11 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-50$ modulo $13$ is $11.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50+11≡-39≡(-3)\\cdot 13≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cab45c4-7a25-50f7-a9f8-9b1a3f5031b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cab45c4-7a25-50f7-a9f8-9b1a3f5031b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415343} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e561b0b-c7fb-5747-abe8-d7ea779b0e25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e204021-2fb2-5d43-b1c6-38cf42c71b93" :question/math-academy-id "q-213336" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $17,$ the additive inverse of $34$ modulo $17$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $34$ modulo $17$ is the integer $0\\le x<17$ such that\n\n$$\nx+34≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17).\n$$\n\nFirst, note that $34≡2\\cdot 17≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17),$ and $0+0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17).$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $34$ modulo $17$ is $0.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n34+0≡2\\cdot 17+0≡2\\cdot 0+0≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17)\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b94962a3-ed6e-58a2-9482-b74058c1d19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94962a3-ed6e-58a2-9482-b74058c1d19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415350} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fd20b03-8c01-539a-b9a3-c55176c9eeeb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6be34eb0-fa03-58ac-aac3-09af6a99143a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213339" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $9,$ the additive inverse of $15$ modulo $9$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $15$ modulo $9$ is the integer $0\\le x<9$ such that\n\n$$\nx+15≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $15≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & ≡1\\cdot 9+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-6+6≡0,$ so $x≡-6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡9-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $15$ modulo $9$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15+3≡18≡2\\cdot 9≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10052852-07da-56bc-9521-67e94277957f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10052852-07da-56bc-9521-67e94277957f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415321} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc7420b-eefc-5772-8a0d-ae7e3535511b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71d1db00-def0-5962-9ebf-e72a766990a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-213340" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16,$ the additive inverse of $28$ modulo $16$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $28$ modulo $16$ is the integer $0\\le x<16$ such that\n\n$$\nx+28≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $28≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & ≡1\\cdot 16+12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-12+12≡0,$ so $x≡-12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡0-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡16-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $28$ modulo $16$ is $4.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28+4≡32≡2\\cdot 16≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8198a59-dcc6-5fda-9c23-786c260cdb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8198a59-dcc6-5fda-9c23-786c260cdb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415356} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b412ab-e118-5b08-8e02-e1a6b6b3e96f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03d7746d-d7c7-51a7-a5bf-9cade8e1e535" :question/math-academy-id "q-213345" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $8,$ the additive inverse of $19$ modulo $8$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $19$ modulo $8$ is the integer $0\\le x<8$ such that\n\n$$\nx+19≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $19≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & ≡2\\cdot 8+3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-3+3≡0,$ so $x≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0-3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8-3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $19$ modulo $8$ is $5.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19+5≡24≡3\\cdot 8≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c91373a9-4a84-52ea-a77a-16beabf09acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d52a20-378b-52e5-96e5-793f517c7e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10d52a20-378b-52e5-96e5-793f517c7e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415322} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a8f06a-eccd-5bc5-98d8-8850779fc609" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "087515b4-1dee-5eec-87da-466a8e426d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-213350" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $14,$ the additive inverse of $-10$ modulo $14$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $-10$ modulo $14$ is the integer $0\\le x<14$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-10)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).\n$$\n\nNotice that $10+(-10)≡0,$ so $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14),$ which is already in the required form. \n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-10$ modulo $14$ is $10.$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c91373a9-4a84-52ea-a77a-16beabf09acb" 