#uuid "7377ff68-7ffb-5b26-80bb-0cac6b01660a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415334} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91b14cb-9165-5a96-9804-4f9358ea91de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3323609a-bf83-5586-b0db-68ed80775b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-213391" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $42,$ the additive inverse of $29$ modulo $42$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $29$ modulo $42$ is the integer $0\\le x<42$ such that\n\n$$\nx+29≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,42).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-29+29≡0,$ so $x≡-29\\,(\\text{mod}\\,32).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42) \\\\  & ≡0-29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42) \\\\  & ≡42-29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $19$ modulo $42$ is $13.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n13+29≡42≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,42)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$"}]}
